DESCRIPTION:We the People\nSunday\, Nov 1 2026 \n4:15 pm pre-concert talk\n
 5:00 pm performance \nAmerica is a tune. It must be sung together. – Gerald
  Stanley Lee\, Crowds: A Moving-Picture of Democracy\, 1913 \nOn Sunday\, N
 ovember 1\, the Bloomington Symphony Orchestra will perform a concert in re
 cognition of our country’s 250th year. From the familiar square dance feel 
 of Aaron Copland’s Hoedown to the soulful\, bluesy tonalities of William Gr
 ant Still’s Afro-American Symphony\, this concert explores what it means to
  be an American. \nProgram: \nHoedown from Rodeo – Aaron Copland\nIn Memori
 am Sojourner Truth – Gary Powell Nash\nAll Things Majestic – Jennifer Higdo
 n\nSymphony No. 1 – Afro-American Symphony – William Grant Still \n  \nI tr
 avel the world\, and I’m happy to say that America is still the great melti
 ng pot – maybe a chunky stew rather than a melting pot at this point\, but 
 you know what I mean. – Philip Glass\, 1999 NPR interview \n\n\n\n\n\n5pm C
 oncert\nBuskirk-Chumley Theater\n\nIncluded as part of your Season Pass. Le
 arn more here.\nIndividual tickets on sale in September.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260904T214629Z
LOCATION:Buskirk-Chumley Theater\, 114 E. Kirkwood Avenue\, Bloomington\, I
 N\, 47408\, United States
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:We the People - Concert at Buskirk-Chumley Theater
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
